+# Combo variant of b99e7126 (index 19)
+# combo_spec: D3/1+D9/1
+# Name: 放大图案铺满网格 + 输出扩展
+
+from functools import reduce
+from itertools import combinations
+from math import gcd
+import random
+
+import numpy as np
+
+from arc_utils import paste
+
+# Design analysis for the fused variant
+# ------------------------------------
+# a) Concrete behavior:
+#    Original b99e7126 used the minority cell's 3x3 interior as a 3x3 mask placed
+#    somewhere inside a 7x7 cell grid; each source pixel became exactly one cell.
+#    Here the same minority mask is still first localized from the sparse input subset,
+#    but the completed pattern is then blown up to a full grid-sized footprint:
+#    each source pixel expands into a proportional band of cells, and that footprint
+#    is anchored at the inferred minority block's top-left. Example with n=7, top=2:
+#    the old task only filled rows 2..4 of the cell grid, while the new task fills a
+#    7-row-tall proportional copy starting at row 2, so rows 2..8 are covered and the
+#    canvas must expand to keep rows 7..8.
+# b) Complexity calibration:
+#    The source mask is still only 3x3 and uses 6-7 active pixels, so the relation
+#    between the tiny source and the large target remains visible. We keep the original
+#    unique-placement constraint on the sparse minority subset so the task is not
+#    ambiguous, and we force expansion by avoiding top=left=0 so D9/1 is actually used.
+# c) Implicit assumptions that changed:
+#    - The old solver assumed the completed footprint was exactly mh x mw cells.
+#      That no longer holds; the new footprint is n x n cells.
+#    - The old solver assumed output shape equals input shape.
+#      That no longer holds; output may grow to (top+n) x (left+n) cells.
+#    - The old solver could update a copy of the input grid in place.
+#      That no longer holds; we rebuild a larger grid from scratch when needed.
+# d) Placement legality:
+#    Input placement still uses the original 3x3 mask window, so minority cells never
+#    overlap and always stay within the 7x7 input grid. The expanded output footprint is
+#    allowed to exceed the original bottom/right boundary, and the output canvas is sized
+#    to exactly contain it.
+# e) Visual observability:
+#    Across examples, the few minority cells in the input identify both the 3x3 source
+#    mask and its top-left anchor. In the output, the same motif becomes large bands of
+#    minority/majority cells, so the "small blueprint -> large proportional stamp" rule
+#    is visible without relying on numeric color ids.
+# f) Visual cleanliness:
+#    Only two cell interiors are used (majority/minority), with separator lines left
+#    intact. Expansion happens only on the lower/right side, so the result remains a
+#    clean rectilinear grid rather than a multi-layer overlap.
+# g) Learnability of color use:
+#    The active color is simply the more frequent color inside the minority cell, exactly
+#    as in the source task. No hidden color ordering is introduced; the enlarged output
+#    merely repeats minority vs majority cells according to the scaled mask.
